<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> there's no single best snowboard brand. Every major maker builds boards for beginners, park riders, and powder hunters, so the right brand is the one whose lineup fits how and where you ride. This guide covers the major makers and what each is known for, so you can pick a starting point.</p></div>

<h2>How to pick a brand</h2>
<p>Brand matters less than the board itself. Length, width, flex, profile, and shape decide how a board rides, and every brand below sells options across that range. Use brand as a way to narrow your search, not as a verdict on quality.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Start with your riding style.</strong> Some brands grew out of backcountry and big-mountain riding, others out of park and street riding. Their lineups still lean that way.</li>
  <li><strong>Get your size first.</strong> Use the <%= link_to "snowboard size calculator", snowboard_size_chart_path %> to find your length range before you shop, then compare boards within it.</li>
  <li><strong>Understand profiles.</strong> Brands use their own names for camber, rocker, and hybrid shapes. Our <%= link_to "camber vs rocker guide", guide_path("camber-vs-rocker") %> explains what those shapes do so the marketing names make sense.</li>
  <li><strong>Consider where it's made.</strong> If it matters to you, some brands build boards in their own factories in the US or Europe. Others contract production.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The major snowboard brands</h2>

<h3>Burton</h3>
<p>Jake Burton Carpenter founded Burton in Vermont in 1977, and the company moved its headquarters to Burlington, Vermont, in 1992. It is one of the brands most closely tied to the growth of snowboarding as a sport. Burton makes boards, boots, bindings, and outerwear, with a lineup that runs from first-timer boards to freeride and park models. <%= gear_link "Burton snowboards", "burton snowboard" %></p>

<h3>Lib Tech</h3>
<p>Lib Tech is a brand of Mervin Manufacturing, a company founded in 1977 by snowboarders Mike Olson and Pete Saari. Mervin builds its boards at its own factory in Sequim, Washington, which has operated since 1996. Lib Tech is known for Magne-Traction, a wavy, serrated edge design meant to grip on hard snow and ice. <%= gear_link "Lib Tech snowboards", "lib tech snowboard" %></p>

<h3>GNU</h3>
<p>GNU is Lib Tech's sister brand under Mervin Manufacturing, and its boards are built in the same Sequim, Washington factory. Many GNU models also use Magne-Traction edges. It's worth comparing GNU and Lib Tech side by side, since they share a factory but have their own lineups and designs. <%= gear_link "GNU snowboards", "gnu snowboard" %></p>

<h3>Jones</h3>
<p>Big-mountain snowboarder Jeremy Jones started Jones Snowboards in 2009, after leaving his longtime sponsor, to make boards for big-mountain and backcountry riding. Its North American headquarters is in Truckee, California, and the brand is part of the Nidecker Group. Jones is a common starting point for riders who spend time off-piste or in powder. <%= gear_link "Jones snowboards", "jones snowboard" %></p>

<h3>CAPiTA</h3>
<p>CAPiTA was started in Seattle around 2000 by Blue Montgomery and Jason Brown. The brand makes its boards at its own factory in Austria, which it calls The Mothership, built on the site of a former Elan factory. That puts it alongside Mervin and Never Summer as a brand on this list that builds boards in a factory it runs itself. <%= gear_link "CAPiTA snowboards", "capita snowboard" %></p>

<h3>Ride</h3>
<p>Ride was founded in Washington State in 1992 by Roger Madison, James Salter, and Tim Pogue, and its first boards hit the market in the 1992–93 season. It grew quickly in the 1990s and remains a widely available brand of boards, boots, and bindings. <%= gear_link "Ride snowboards", "ride snowboard" %></p>

<h3>K2</h3>
<p>Brothers Bill and Don Kirschner founded K2 on Vashon Island, Washington, in 1962, and the company made its name with early fiberglass skis. K2 moved its business office to Seattle in 2006 and sells snowboards alongside skis. Because it makes both, K2 is a familiar name in shops and rental fleets. <%= gear_link "K2 snowboards", "k2 snowboard" %></p>

<h3>Salomon</h3>
<p>Salomon was founded in Annecy, in the French Alps, in 1947 as a maker of ski edges, and it is now part of Amer Sports. It released its first snowboard in 1997 after years of development. Salomon makes boards, boots, and bindings, so it's an easy brand to buy as a matched setup. <%= gear_link "Salomon snowboards", "salomon snowboard" %></p>

<h3>Rome</h3>
<p>Rome was founded in 2001 in Waterbury, Vermont, by Josh Reid and Paul Maravetz, who had previously worked at Burton. It started out as Rome SDS, short for Snowboard Design Syndicate, and has kept its headquarters in Waterbury. Rome makes boards and bindings. <%= gear_link "Rome snowboards", "rome snowboard" %></p>

<h3>Never Summer</h3>
<p>Never Summer traces its roots to 1983, when brothers Tim and Tracey Canaday started their first snowboard company. The Never Summer name dates from 1991, and the company builds its boards in its own factory in Denver, Colorado. It is known for its rocker-camber hybrid profiles, which put rocker between the feet and camber under them. <%= gear_link "Never Summer snowboards", "never summer snowboard" %></p>

<h3>Arbor</h3>
<p>Bob Carlson and Chris Jensen founded Arbor in Venice, California, in 1995, and the brand is still headquartered there. Its first boards used Hawaiian koa wood, and wood topsheets and a focus on sustainability remain central to its identity. Arbor also runs a reforestation program. <%= gear_link "Arbor snowboards", "arbor snowboard" %></p>

<h3>YES.</h3>
<p>Pro riders David Carrier Porcheron (DCP), Romain De Marchi, and JP Solberg started YES. in 2009 after leaving Burton. The brand launched in partnership with Nidecker, the Swiss company whose group also includes Jones. It's a good example of a rider-founded brand, shaped by the pros who started it. <%= gear_link "YES. snowboards", "yes snowboard" %></p>

